-- title:
--   Measurable selection of the $n$th element of an infinite subset of the naturals
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a measurable space and let $p_s\subseteq\mathbb N$ be infinite for every $s\in S$. Assume $\{s:j\in p_s\}$ is measurable for each $j$. For every fixed $n\in\mathbb N$, the function selecting the $n$th element in increasing order is measurable:
--   $$s\longmapsto\operatorname{nth}(p_s,n).$$
--   This allows zero vectors to be removed measurably from a Gram–Schmidt sequence.
-- source:
--   Ruijun Wang, Comparisons of benchmark equivalence relations, Theorem 1.5 (formerly MeasureEquivalence.tex, Theorem 1.3); supporting declaration MeasureUnitary.measurable_nat_nth in MeasureGramSchmidtBorel.lean, lines 57–73; local source SHA-256 7ea95ae5a507f10812fc04e774d16f379f04128c5fb636ebbfc691a7e6f579ce. Designated upload repository: https://github.com/RayjunWang/Benchmark (no source commit published yet).

